      "synthesis": "The evidence supports that dense retrieval is vulnerable to domain shift, as BEIR-2021 finds BM25 achieves a strong zero-shot median nDCG@10 of 41.9 across diverse domains and most dense models trained on MS MARCO fail to consistently beat it out-of-domain; this failure mode is identified as the dominant axis for learned retrieval systems. DPR-2020 notes that dense retrieval underperforms BM25 on entity-heavy, out-of-domain queries, suggesting poor generalization when query semantics diverge from training data. The pack does not evaluate internal wiki corpora or codename-heavy query distributions specifically, so we cannot confirm whether the domain shift risk is mitigated by closed-domain content or amplified by jargon and product codenames. A key contradiction exists between BEIR-2021's finding that dense models fail out-of-domain and DPR-2020’s claim of superior performance on Natural Questions — a benchmark with entity-heavy queries — which may reflect training data mismatch rather than generalization failure. This tension complicates whether dense retrieval is robust in our closed-domain but codename-heavy setting.",
